From fcc82fd5961d9d11b5c1510a125c1cadded592c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 08:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Ask Apple once for a new machine, and say what it answered

A sign-in that needs a new anisette machine provisions it from this iPhone
straight to Apple's servers (gsa.apple.com: its lookup, start and finish),
whichever anisette server signs it. When Apple answered it with an error,
CartoucheAnisette went on with the next of the Cartouche Installer's
servers, and the next: a sign-in that Apple's provisioning refused could
send Apple the same requests four times in a few seconds, and a player who
tried twice more, up to twelve times in under three minutes, none of which
got a machine. Another server asks Apple the same, from the same iPhone. As
the Cartouche Installer, which asks one server, Apple's answer now ends the
sign-in: an HTTP error from any step of its provisioning, or an answer of
Apple's that cannot be read, as AnisetteKit's words name them. A server
that does not answer, or whose own answer cannot be read, still hands on
to the next.

CartoucheOutcome tells Apple's answer apart, as family provisioning, kind
httpError or invalidResponse, with the HTTP status, service provisioning
and the step that answered as reason (lookup, start, finish). It left
family other, kind AnisetteError and no status, and Cartouche showed
AnisetteKit's English words.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MzPJouaxDEHiaAYJRW7jTY
